[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"AWS Shield: DDoS Protection on AWS"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"AWS Shield is a managed Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) protection service that safeguards applications running on the AWS cloud. It provides always-on detection and automatic inline mitigations that minimize application downtime and latency. AWS Shield comes in two tiers: Standard and Advanced."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"AWS Shield Standard"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"AWS Shield Standard is automatically enabled for all AWS customers at no additional charge. It protects against the most common, frequently occurring network and transport layer DDoS attacks that target your website or applications. Shield Standard defends against attacks like SYN/UDP floods, and HTTP floods."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Automatic protection: No configuration required; it's always on."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Protects against common infrastructure layer attacks."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"No additional cost."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"AWS Shield Advanced"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"AWS Shield Advanced provides enhanced DDoS protection for your applications running on Amazon EC2, Elastic Load Balancing (ELB), Amazon CloudFront, AWS Global Accelerator, and Route 53. In addition to the network and transport layer protections of Standard, Shield Advanced provides detection and mitigation against more sophisticated and larger attacks. It also gives you 24x7 access to the AWS DDoS Response Team (DRT) and cost protection against usage spikes due to DDoS attacks."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Enhanced detection and mitigation capabilities."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"24x7 access to the AWS DDoS Response Team (DRT)."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Cost protection against usage spikes during DDoS attacks."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Integration with AWS WAF (Web Application Firewall) for application layer protection."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring AWS Shield Advanced"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"To configure AWS Shield Advanced, you need to subscribe to the service and associate it with the AWS resources you want to protect. Here's a general outline of the steps involved:"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Subscribe to AWS Shield Advanced through the AWS Management Console."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Define protected resources: Select the EC2 instances, ELB load balancers, CloudFront distributions, Global Accelerator accelerators, or Route 53 hosted zones you want to protect."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Configure mitigation settings: Customize mitigation strategies based on your application's needs. This may involve setting up AWS WAF rules or configuring custom mitigations with the DRT."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Monitor and analyze: Use the AWS Shield console and CloudWatch metrics to monitor your application's traffic and the effectiveness of the DDoS protections."},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Integrating AWS Shield with AWS WAF"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"AWS Shield Advanced integrates seamlessly with AWS WAF to provide comprehensive protection against both network and application layer DDoS attacks. AWS WAF allows you to define custom rules to filter malicious traffic based on criteria such as IP addresses, HTTP headers, and request body content."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"Example AWS WAF rule (JSON):
{
  "Name": "BlockMaliciousIPs",
  "Priority": 1,
  "Action": {
    "Type": "BLOCK"
  },
  "Statement": {
    "IPSetReferenceStatement": {
      "ARN": "arn:aws:wafv2:us-east-1:123456789012:regional/ipset/MaliciousIPs/xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx"
    }
  },
  "VisibilityConfig": {
    "SampledRequestsEnabled": true,
    "CloudWatchMetricsEnabled": true,
    "MetricName": "BlockMaliciousIPsMetric"
  }
}"},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for DDoS Protection"},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use AWS Shield Standard for basic protection against common DDoS attacks."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Consider AWS Shield Advanced for applications that require enhanced protection and 24x7 support."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Integrate AWS Shield with AWS WAF to protect against application layer attacks."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Regularly review and update your AWS WAF rules to address new threats."},{"order":28,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Monitor your application's traffic and performance using CloudWatch metrics and AWS Shield console."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Establish a DDoS incident response plan and regularly test it."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"AWS Shield provides a robust and scalable solution for protecting your applications against DDoS attacks. By understanding the different tiers of Shield and following best practices, you can ensure the availability and performance of your applications on the AWS cloud."}]
